- op: create  # adce
  at: '2026-07-16T19:53:54Z'  # adce
  by: agent  # adce
  actor: jgstern_agent  # adce
  clock: 1  # adce
  nonce: adce  # adce
  data:  # adce
    kind: work_item  # adce
    title: "Go cross-package call to in-tree (go.mod-local) function resolves to external_symbol stub for some import paths"  # adce
    status: todo_hard  # adce
    priority: 3  # adce
    parent:  # adce
    tags:  # adce
    - dogfood  # adce
    - wave5  # adce
    - go  # adce
    isbefore: []  # adce
    duplicate_of: []  # adce
    not_duplicate_of: []  # adce
    pr_ref:  # adce
    description: "Surfaced while validating the Caddy dispatch linker (WI-zizig, PR #693) on real Caddy (/tmp/f5_cohort_pool/caddy). A cross-package call like gzip.go's 'caddy.RegisterModule(...)' — where the imported package is in the SAME go module — sometimes resolves to a 'go:<importpath>:0-0:RegisterModule:external_symbol' stub instead of the in-tree function node, breaking the reachability chain (init -> RegisterModule -> Module.CaddyModule -> ...). Measured: on real Caddy, 120/136 (88%) CaddyModule marker methods have an incoming call/dispatches_to edge (chain resolves in-tree), but 16/136 (12%) do not — those modules' handlers stay unreachable from the registration flow even with the WI-zizig linker's edges present. A bare synthetic fixture WITHOUT go.mod resolves 0% (fake import path 'example.com/caddy' -> external), confirming the resolver keys on module-path mapping. Action: investigate which of the 16 real-Caddy modules fail and why (package layout? internal/ packages? vendored? alias imports?), and whether the Go analyzer's import->in-tree-package mapping can be tightened. Distinct from WI-zizig (that linker's marker->handler edges are correct; this is the upstream init->RegisterModule resolution)."  # adce
    fields: {}  # adce
- op: discuss  # 9306
  at: '2026-08-11T19:36:09Z'  # 9306
  by: agent  # 9306
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9306
  clock: 2  # 9306
  nonce: '9306'  # 9306
  message: "LIKELY ROOT CAUSE NAMED, 2026-08-11 — this item's own 'alias imports?' hypothesis, now with a mechanism and a control. Filed as INV-javid-zubam-tajil-sajis-loran-tinik-mitod-baman.\n\ngo.py derives a package alias as the LAST segment of the import path. Caddy's module path is github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2, so the alias binds as the literal 'v2' and 'caddy' is never registered — every 'caddy.RegisterModule(...)' then falls to the external placeholder, which is exactly this item's reported symptom (init -> RegisterModule chain broken, handlers unreachable).\n\nPOSITIVE/NEGATIVE PAIR, one file, one analyze_go run, modelled on this item's own gzip.go example:\n  import github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2                   -> alias 'v2'\n    caddy.RegisterModule(Gzip{})\n      -> go:external:0-0:RegisterModule:unresolved                       LOST\n  import github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2/modules/caddyhttp  -> alias 'caddyhttp'\n    caddyhttp.RegisterHandler(Gzip{})\n      -> go:github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2/modules/caddyhttp:0-0:RegisterHandler:unresolved   RESOLVED\nThe DEEPER path resolves and the MODULE ROOT does not. That asymmetry is the signature of the alias derivation rather than of anything about the callee, and it rules out the item's other listed hypotheses (package layout, internal/, vendoring) for this shape at least.\n\nNOT CLAIMED: that this accounts for all 16 of 136. If every registration went through the root package, all 136 would fail rather than 16 — so either the WI-zizig linker masks most of them or a second mechanism is present. Checking the 16 against real Caddy is the next step and was NOT done. This note names a mechanism that produces the symptom; it does not close the item."  # 9306
- op: discuss  # 2f38
  at: '2026-08-11T21:13:08Z'  # 2f38
  by: agent  # 2f38
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2f38
  clock: 3  # 2f38
  nonce: 2f38  # 2f38
  message: "UPSTREAM CAUSE FIXED AND MERGED (PR #293, dev 0ca355eddb). This item is NOT closed — read why.\n\nINV-javid shipped: go.py no longer derives a package alias as the bare last path element, so a /vN module path binds its real identifier. Verified on merged dev with the caddy-shaped fixture modelled on this item's own gzip.go example:\n\n  caddy.RegisterModule(Gzip{})\n    BEFORE  go:external:0-0:RegisterModule:unresolved\n    AFTER   go:github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2:0-0:RegisterModule:unresolved\n\nOn real caddy the paired A/B moves 172 call sites: 71 recover an import path and 101 now resolve to an IN-REPO symbol (function 12, method 6, type 83), with the calls-edge set identical across arms and zero regressions.\n\nWHY THIS DOES NOT CLOSE THE ITEM. What is established is that the import path is recovered — the PRECONDITION for the init -> RegisterModule -> Module.CaddyModule chain this item reports as broken. What is NOT established is that the 16 of 136 unreachable marker methods are now reachable. That was not measured, and the arithmetic argues against a clean 1:1: if every registration went through the root 'caddy' package, all 136 would have failed rather than 16 — so either the WI-zizig linker was already masking most of them, or a second mechanism is present, or the 16 fail for a different reason entirely.\n\nTO CLOSE THIS ITEM, re-run its own measurement on real caddy against merged dev and report the marker-method reachability count. If it is still 16, this was a real fix to a different problem and the item's hypothesis list ('package layout? internal/ packages? vendored? alias imports?') should have 'alias imports' struck rather than confirmed. I did not run it.\n\nAlso worth noting for whoever does: 83 of caddy's 101 in-repo landings are kind='type' — Go type conversions like caddy.Duration(x), which are calls only syntactically. Those edges were already 'calls' edges pointing at external; the fix makes their target accurate and thereby surfaces a pre-existing modelling question that belongs to WI-jalal's family (a calls edge whose dst is not callable), not to this item."  # 2f38
