    description: "[REVIEW 2026-11-01, MIS-PREMISED] 2026-11-01 review (dev 100b6fbb6e, caddy df65455): survey still gives 120/136 CaddyModule methods with incoming calls/dispatches_to - the same 16 after INV-javid. init->RegisterModule resolves (87 calls to in-tree modules.go RegisterModule). 15 of the 16 are non-struct named types (MatchHost/MatchPath/VarsMatcher/PEMLoader/FileLoader/...); all 119 reachable are structs. go.py attaches implicit-satisfaction base_classes only to kind=='struct'. The 16th, ListenerWrapper, shares its name with an interface (not verified). (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\nSurfaced while validating the Caddy dispatch linker (WI-zizig, PR #693) on real Caddy (/tmp/f5_cohort_pool/caddy). A cross-package call like gzip.go's 'caddy.RegisterModule(...)' — where the imported package is in the SAME go module — sometimes resolves to a 'go:<importpath>:0-0:RegisterModule:external_symbol' stub instead of the in-tree function node, breaking the reachability chain (init -> RegisterModule -> Module.CaddyModule -> ...). Measured: on real Caddy, 120/136 (88%) CaddyModule marker methods have an incoming call/dispatches_to edge (chain resolves in-tree), but 16/136 (12%) do not — those modules' handlers stay unreachable from the registration flow even with the WI-zizig linker's edges present. A bare synthetic fixture WITHOUT go.mod resolves 0% (fake import path 'example.com/caddy' -> external), confirming the resolver keys on module-path mapping. Action: investigate which of the 16 real-Caddy modules fail and why (package layout? internal/ packages? vendored? alias imports?), and whether the Go analyzer's import->in-tree-package mapping can be tightened. Distinct from WI-zizig (that linker's marker->handler edges are correct; this is the upstream init->RegisterModule resolution)."  # a46a
